# `h.call(o, 1)` is a call by name on what lies at h. If a set of two closures were an unknown function there, the receiver and every argument would be handed to code that nobody sees: one listener more, and an emitter is out of sight.
# Each member is asked as the one closure is asked: Function.prototype.call enters it with the shifted arguments.
# function take(h, o) { return h.call(o, 1); }  const o = { x: 1 };  take(first, o);  take(second, o);
